{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"parsing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parsing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parsing is synchronous and deterministic:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { parseMarkdown } from '@tanstack/markdown/parser'\n\nconst document = parseMarkdown(source, {\n  frontmatter: true,\n  headingIds: true,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"input-normalization","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Input normalization"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The parser normalizes line endings before extracting frontmatter, link definitions, footnote definitions, blocks, and inline content. It does not read files, load plugins, or perform asynchronous work."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"frontmatter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Frontmatter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A leading "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"---"},{"type":"text","value":" block is extracted by default:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"---\ntitle: Small Markdown\npublished: true\n---\n\n# Article","lang":"md"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"document.frontmatter"},{"type":"text","value":" contains the unparsed text between delimiters. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"frontmatter: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to treat the input as ordinary Markdown."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"heading-ids","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Heading IDs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Headings receive lowercase, duplicate-safe IDs by default. Disable them or provide a custom function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"parseMarkdown(source, { headingIds: false })\n\nparseMarkdown(source, {\n  headingIds(text, sourceLine) {\n    return `${sourceLine}-${text.toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ', '-')}`\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second callback argument is the zero-based normalized parser line index. It is not a source-location API because frontmatter and definitions may already have been extracted."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"definitions-and-footnotes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definitions and footnotes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Link definitions are collected before block parsing. Reference labels are matched case-insensitively with whitespace collapsed, and the first definition wins when labels repeat. Footnotes are emitted in first-reference order, with collision-safe IDs and back-reference counts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"references"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footnotes"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footnoteOrder"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footnoteCounts"},{"type":"text","value":" fields in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ParseOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" carry parser state through nested parsing. Most consumers should leave them unset."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"extensions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extensions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extensions may claim a block, transform inline nodes, transform the completed document, or render custom HTML. They run in array order. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/extensions.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extensions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for lifecycle and examples."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"complexity-bounds","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complexity bounds"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Block recursion is capped, inline scanning uses explicit budgets, and malformed-input behavior is covered by fixed-seed fuzzing and adversarial runtime tests. Inputs beyond a parser depth limit degrade to text content instead of recursing without bound."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"parsing","text":"Parsing","level":1},{"id":"input-normalization","text":"Input normalization","level":2},{"id":"frontmatter","text":"Frontmatter","level":2},{"id":"heading-ids","text":"Heading IDs","level":2},{"id":"definitions-and-footnotes","text":"Definitions and footnotes","level":2},{"id":"extensions","text":"Extensions","level":2},{"id":"complexity-bounds","text":"Complexity bounds","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Parsing\n\nParsing is synchronous and deterministic:\n\n```ts\nimport { parseMarkdown } from '@tanstack/markdown/parser'\n\nconst document = parseMarkdown(source, {\n  frontmatter: true,\n  headingIds: true,\n})\n```\n\n## Input normalization\n\nThe parser normalizes line endings before extracting frontmatter, link definitions, footnote definitions, blocks, and inline content. It does not read files, load plugins, or perform asynchronous work.\n\n## Frontmatter\n\nA leading `---` block is extracted by default:\n\n```md\n---\ntitle: Small Markdown\npublished: true\n---\n\n# Article\n```\n\n`document.frontmatter` contains the unparsed text between delimiters. Set `frontmatter: false` to treat the input as ordinary Markdown.\n\n## Heading IDs\n\nHeadings receive lowercase, duplicate-safe IDs by default. Disable them or provide a custom function:\n\n```ts\nparseMarkdown(source, { headingIds: false })\n\nparseMarkdown(source, {\n  headingIds(text, sourceLine) {\n    return `${sourceLine}-${text.toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ', '-')}`\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe second callback argument is the zero-based normalized parser line index. It is not a source-location API because frontmatter and definitions may already have been extracted.\n\n## Definitions and footnotes\n\nLink definitions are collected before block parsing. Reference labels are matched case-insensitively with whitespace collapsed, and the first definition wins when labels repeat. Footnotes are emitted in first-reference order, with collision-safe IDs and back-reference counts.\n\nThe `references`, `footnotes`, `footnoteOrder`, and `footnoteCounts` fields in `ParseOptions` carry parser state through nested parsing. Most consumers should leave them unset.\n\n## Extensions\n\nExtensions may claim a block, transform inline nodes, transform the completed document, or render custom HTML. They run in array order. See [Extensions](../guides/extensions.md) for lifecycle and examples.\n\n## Complexity bounds\n\nBlock recursion is capped, inline scanning uses explicit budgets, and malformed-input behavior is covered by fixed-seed fuzzing and adversarial runtime tests. Inputs beyond a parser depth limit degrade to text content instead of recursing without bound.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Parsing\n---\n\n# Parsing\n\nParsing is synchronous and deterministic:\n\n```ts\nimport { parseMarkdown } from '@tanstack/markdown/parser'\n\nconst document = parseMarkdown(source, {\n  frontmatter: true,\n  headingIds: true,\n})\n```\n\n## Input normalization\n\nThe parser normalizes line endings before extracting frontmatter, link definitions, footnote definitions, blocks, and inline content. It does not read files, load plugins, or perform asynchronous work.\n\n## Frontmatter\n\nA leading `---` block is extracted by default:\n\n```md\n---\ntitle: Small Markdown\npublished: true\n---\n\n# Article\n```\n\n`document.frontmatter` contains the unparsed text between delimiters. Set `frontmatter: false` to treat the input as ordinary Markdown.\n\n## Heading IDs\n\nHeadings receive lowercase, duplicate-safe IDs by default. Disable them or provide a custom function:\n\n```ts\nparseMarkdown(source, { headingIds: false })\n\nparseMarkdown(source, {\n  headingIds(text, sourceLine) {\n    return `${sourceLine}-${text.toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ', '-')}`\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe second callback argument is the zero-based normalized parser line index. It is not a source-location API because frontmatter and definitions may already have been extracted.\n\n## Definitions and footnotes\n\nLink definitions are collected before block parsing. Reference labels are matched case-insensitively with whitespace collapsed, and the first definition wins when labels repeat. Footnotes are emitted in first-reference order, with collision-safe IDs and back-reference counts.\n\nThe `references`, `footnotes`, `footnoteOrder`, and `footnoteCounts` fields in `ParseOptions` carry parser state through nested parsing. Most consumers should leave them unset.\n\n## Extensions\n\nExtensions may claim a block, transform inline nodes, transform the completed document, or render custom HTML. They run in array order. See [Extensions](../guides/extensions.md) for lifecycle and examples.\n\n## Complexity bounds\n\nBlock recursion is capped, inline scanning uses explicit budgets, and malformed-input behavior is covered by fixed-seed fuzzing and adversarial runtime tests. Inputs beyond a parser depth limit degrade to text content instead of recursing without bound.\n"}
